Falsifies `direct_memory_access_validator::in_region_is_sound`.

The defect: `is_in_region` checks the buffer's start against the region and stops
there, dropping `end <= limit`. This is the whole confinement reduced to a
half-check, and it is the plausible half to lose: a driver's buffers all begin
inside its own region, so every honest workload still works, every hand-written
test with an in-region base still passes, and the escape is a *length* rather than
an address. A device handed `addr = base + size - 8` with a 4 KiB length reads or
writes 4 KiB past the end of the grant.

Expected to fail: `assert!(end <= limit)` in `in_region_is_sound`. Confirmed:
Kani reports exactly one failing check, described as `assertion failed: end <=
limit`, with the witness `base = 0, size = u64::MAX - 1, addr = 0, len =
u64::MAX`. The two `assert!(addr >= base)` statements around it stay green, which
is what makes this a falsification of the containment claim rather than of the
harness.

diff --git a/crates/direct_memory_access_validator/src/lib.rs b/crates/direct_memory_access_validator/src/lib.rs
index 9a6d0500..01b883c1 100644
--- a/crates/direct_memory_access_validator/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/direct_memory_access_validator/src/lib.rs
@@ -40,7 +40,7 @@ pub const F_INDIRECT: u16 = 4;
 /// See the `in_region_is_sound` proof and notes/dma/validator.md.
 pub fn is_in_region(base: u64, size: u64, addr: u64, len: u64) -> bool {
     match (addr.checked_add(len), base.checked_add(size)) {
-        (Some(end), Some(limit)) => addr >= base && end <= limit,
+        (Some(_end), Some(_limit)) => addr >= base,
         _ => false,
     }
 }
